;;; Two activators of one rule, an `int` and a nested fact in one position,
;;; lost a derivation — M1e2 **W7**, register row `W7/binding-key-layout`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), Q-M1e.16; fixed by S1e2.2.7 (2026-11-04).
;;; Promoted from `corpus/fuzz_findings/binding-key-layout.ein`, which stays
;;; there as a frontend-fuzzer seed.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `defined_behaviour.md` § 3.2's thirteen lines, as M1e S1e.1.4 wrote
;;; them. `(note edge 1)` binds `?R` and `?f`; `(note edge (src Y))` binds
;;; `?R` alone, since a nested fact binds no parameter, and leaves `?f` to
;;; `(holds ?f)`. `binding-key-swapped.ein` is this file with the two
;;; activator lines exchanged, and that pair is the test: the order two
;;; facts are written in may not change what is derived.
;;; `binding-key-int-only.ein` and `binding-key-nested-only.ein` keep one
;;; activator each.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The nested-fact activator alone derives `(noted ?a ?f)` for every edge
;;; source `?a` (1, 2) and every `?f` that holds; the int activator alone
;;; derives the part of it with `?f` = 1. A rule set with one more
;;; activator cannot conclude less, so root holds `(noted 1 1)`, `(noted 1
;;; 3)`, `(noted 2 1)` and `(noted 2 3)`. `Y`, written only inside `(src Y)`,
;;; is a name of the program (Q-M1e2.9 (a), ruled 2026-11-04), so H is the
;;; one candidate `(holds Y)` and R is two states: ∅, and `{(holds Y)}`,
;;; which adds `(noted 1 Y)` and `(noted 2 Y)`. It is
;;; `binding-key-nested-only.ein`'s R, fact for fact.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.7 a rule application's identity, `(rule, activator,
;;; values)`, interned only an activator's symbol arguments, so both plans
;;; had the activator `[edge]` and their register files, `(?R ?f ?a ?b)` and
;;; `(?R ?a ?b ?f)`, were compared position by position. The nested plan's
;;; match `?a = 1, ?b = 2, ?f = 3` and the int plan's `?f = 1, ?a = 2, ?b =
;;; 3` are the same vector, `(edge 1 2 3)`, so the second was dropped as a
;;; repeat of the first and `(noted 1 3)` was derived in no state (`ein
;;; solve FILE --dump-answer DIR` at `9c3c3fbc`, 2026-11-04). A build with
;;; debug assertions aborted instead, in `check_layout`. Since S1e2.2.7 the
;;; identity interns every argument of the activator, as the compile cache's
;;; key does, so each plan has a space of its own; the `:expect` below
;;; states the answer, and it holds in either build.
;;;
;;; ── Since M1f S1f.5.5's swap ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A run of digits is a name (S1f.5.4's ruling), so `1`, `2` and `3`, the
;;; edges' ends, are candidate objects beside `Y`, and unscoped H is 21
;;; candidates whose R passes the budget (T1f.5.5.9's sweep). The solve below
;;; holds `edge` and `noted` out of the hypotheses, S1f.5.4's proposal A
;;; (accepted 2026-11-08), so H is `(holds 2)` and `(holds Y)` and R is four
;;; states, the two above and each with `(holds 2)` added, which adds `(noted
;;; 1 2)` and `(noted 2 2)`; `ein oracle` gives the same R. The point stands:
;;; root's `noted` facts are the same in either activator order, and `Y` is a
;;; candidate.
;;;
;;; ── Since M1f S1f.5.18 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; An activator binds a parameter to a nested fact as to a name (W33, the
;;; user's ruling of 2026-11-08 on S1f.5.5's census, fixed by S1f.5.18's
;;; T1f.5.18.2 on 2026-11-09). `(note edge (src Y))` binds `?f` to `(src Y)`,
;;; and no `(holds (src Y))` is a fact or a candidate, so that plan derives
;;; nothing; `(note edge 1)` binds `?f` to `1` and derives `(noted 1 1)` and
;;; `(noted 2 1)`. Every state holds those two and no other `noted` fact, in
;;; either activator order. The `:expect` below stated the four, and the two
;;; more each candidate added, that the unbound `?f` derived, W33's wrong
;;; answer, until that commit restated it. The two plans bind `?f` in their
;;; seeds now, so their register layouts are one.

(relation edge  Node Node)
(relation holds Node)
(relation noted Node Node)

(rule note (?R ?f)
  :match  (and (?R ?a ?b) (holds ?f))
  :assert (noted ?a ?f))

(edge 1 2) (edge 2 3)
(holds 1)  (holds 3)

(note edge 1)
(note edge (src Y))

(solve
  ;; S1f.5.4's proposal A (accepted 2026-11-08), at M1f S1f.5.5's swap: a
  ;; run of digits is a name, so `1`, `2` and `3` are candidate objects and
  ;; unscoped H is 21 candidates whose R passes the budget; `edge` and
  ;; `noted` held out, H is `(holds 2)` and `(holds Y)`, the oracle the same.
  :no-hypothesis (edge noted)
  ;; `holds` and `noted` are named, so both are closed: each state lists
  ;; their complete extent, root's facts included.
  :expect (region
            ;; state 1 of 4 — ∅, root itself · minimal
            (model (holds 1) (holds 3) (noted 1 1) (noted 2 1))
            ;; state 2 of 4 — 1 candidate · from {(holds 2)}
            (model (holds 1) (holds 2) (holds 3) (noted 1 1) (noted 2 1))
            ;; state 3 of 4 — 1 candidate · from {(holds Y)}
            (model (holds 1) (holds 3) (holds Y) (noted 1 1) (noted 2 1))
            ;; state 4 of 4 — 2 candidates · maximal · from {(holds 2), (holds Y)}
            (model (holds 1) (holds 2) (holds 3) (holds Y) (noted 1 1) (noted 2 1))))
